sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/aristotle-pa04-19> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Animals that bear few young at a time, whether horned or solid-hoofed, have two mammae in the region of the thighs, while litter-bearing or polydactylous animals have numerous teats along the belly or two in the middle of the abdomen.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Mammae: two at the thighs in animals with few young, many on the belly in litter-bearers";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Aristotle, On the Parts of Animals (De Partibus Animalium), Book IV, Part 10; translated by William Ogle, The Works of Aristotle Translated into English, ed. J. A. Smith and W. D. Ross, Oxford: Clarendon Press, vol. V (1912). Scope: viviparous quadrupeds: horse, ass, camel, deer, ox, goat, swine, dog, lion"
}
